<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Singing Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1987">1987</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Singing Revolution was the movement of mass, largely non-violent protest from 1987 to 1991 that led to the restoration of independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, Latvia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. In Estonia it took its name from huge song gatherings such as the night song festivals of 1988 in Tallinn. Estonia declared its independence restored on 20 August 1991, during the failed coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/singing-revolution.jpg" alt="Singing Revolution" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer Paulius Lileikis. Lithuanian Central State Archives. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leaders_of_the_Supreme_Council_of_Lithuania_on_11_March_1990.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
